        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Recent evidence suggests that exposure to high-linear energy transfer (LET) radiation encountered during deep space travel may be associated with short- or long-term risks of cardiovascular disease. However, these risks are not well defined, and potential gender differences are unknown. We have shown that exposure of male mice to low-dose oxygen ions causes small but significant changes in cardiac function and that both protons and oxygen ions cause dose-dependent increases in the markers of inflammatory cells in the heart. While these studies have started to characterize the effects of low-dose charged particles on cardiac function and structure in the male mouse, the dose rates in these studies were relatively high compared to chronic low-dose rate exposures in space. Therefore, additional work needs to be done in animals chronically exposed to low-dose rate charged particle irradiation. Moreover, the inclusion of additional animal species will greatly enhance the potential for translation of the results into humans compared to research in mouse models alone. Here, we aim to provide insight into the effects of chronic low-dose rate high-LET radiation exposures on the heart and identify how animal species differ in their response to high-LET radiation, addressing the Risk of Cardiovascular Disease and Other Degenerative Tissue Effects from Radiation Exposure and assisting in closure of the Gaps Degen – 1, Degen – 2, and Degen – 3. 
<p>
We pursue three experiments: 1) In collaboration with the Colorado State University Specialized Center of Research on Carcinogenesis, we make use of its long-term studies in male and female mouse models exposed to chronic low-dose rate neutrons to assess cardiac function with echocardiography, and we collect and analyze cardiac tissues obtained from these mice; 2) We perform echocardiography and tissue analysis in rats that are exposed to low-dose rate neutrons in the neutron facility that is part of the Specialized Center of Research on Carcinogenesis; and 3) We assess markers of inflammatory infiltration and mitochondrial morphology and function in cardiac tissue samples of rats and rabbits that had received acute exposures to protons and oxygen ions as part of the Center for Space Radiation Research. These experiments make use of existing animals and tissue samples to aid in defining the risk of cardiovascular disease from space radiation exposure.</p><p>
APPROACH:<br>
These studies aim to characterize the risk of degenerative cardiovascular disease of space radiation, by comparing the effects of exposure to low-dose protons, oxygen ions, and low-dose rate neutrons in mouse, rat, and rabbit models. Experiment 1 makes of female BALB/c mice and male C3H mice. As part of the Colorado State University (CSU) Specialized Center of Research on Carcinogenesis large cohorts of mice are exposed to low-dose rate or high-dose neutrons, starting at the age of 100-120 days, to a total dose of 0.1 Gy, 0.2 Gy, and 0.4 Gy. Subgroups of mice receive aspirin in the drinking water as a potential countermeasure against adverse radiation effects. At the age of 500 days, 10 mice of each experimental group are anesthetized with isoflurane inhalation, and an ultrasound scan of the heart is obtained. At the age of 800 days, this procedure is performed once more. All animals are euthanized at the age of 800 days, and heart tissue collected for histology and western-blot analysis of markers of inflammatory infiltration and mitochondrial function.
</p><p>
For experiment 2, a total of 10 male Long Evans rats are exposed to low-dose rate neutrons in the same chronic neutron facility, starting at the age of 100-120 days, to a total dose of 0.4 Gy. A total of 10 rats receive sham-irradiation. At the age of 500 days, the rats are anesthetized with isoflurane inhalation, and an ultrasound scan of the heart is obtained. At the age of 800 days, this procedure is performed once more. All animals are euthanized at the age of 800 days, and heart tissue collected for histology and western-blot analysis of markers of inflammatory infiltration and mitochondrial function.
</p><p>
For experiment 3, tissues have been obtained from male Long Evans rats and male New Zealand White rabbits that were exposed to oxygen ions (600 MeV/n, 0.08-0.11 Gy/min tot a total dose of 0.5 Gy), protons (250 MeV, 0.10-0.12 Gy/min to a total dose of 0.5 Gy), or sham treatment at the NASA Space Radiation Laboratory (9-11 animals per group) and were followed to 12 months after irradiation. Specimens of left ventricle are used for western-blots to quantify protein markers of leukocytes, T-cells, monocytes/macrophages, extracellular matrix, mitochondrial fission/fusion, and mitochondrial enzyme levels.
</p><p>
RESULTS:<br>
Upon re-evaluation of neutron doses delivered to the mice, it was determined that the lowest radiation dose was 0.12 Gy (instead of the scheduled 0.1 Gy). The other neutron radiation doses were delivered as originally indicated. Progress of analysis of animals and tissue samples was excellent, as outlined below for each Specific Aim.</p>
